⚡🇩🇪🇳🇱🇪🇺 Germany and the Netherlands are outliers with low storage. On August 5, Germany's storage was 47.6% full (normal 75.1%), a 27.5-point gap (-2.06 standard deviations). The Netherlands was worse in raw terms: 38.5% (normal 77.0%), a 38.4-point shortfall.

Germany is the main problem. With 246.5 TWh capacity (second-largest), its 27-point deficit equals -67.4 TWh, the largest absolute hole in European storage, accounting for over a third of the EU-wide shortfall. The Netherlands has the worst percentage deviation (-38 points) but a smaller tank, resulting in a -54.9 TWh absolute gap, second only to Germany's.

